Hotel Booking Cancellation Analysis
Revenue Leakage Detection using Python EDA

Problem Statement
A hotel chain operating two property types — City Hotel and Resort Hotel — recorded a 37% booking cancellation rate across 119,390 bookings from 2015 to 2017. Every last-minute cancellation results in an empty room and zero revenue for that night.
Business Goal: Identify the root causes of cancellations, quantify the revenue impact, and recommend data-driven policy changes to reduce the cancellation rate by at least 10%.
Key Business Questions:

Which booking channels drive the most cancellations?
Does booking lead time affect cancellation likelihood?
How much revenue is being lost due to cancellations?
Which customer segments need policy intervention?


Situation — Action — Result
SituationHotel chain facing 37% cancellation rate causing €16.7M in estimated revenue loss across 2015–2017, with no data-driven policy in place to address it.ActionPerformed end-to-end EDA on 119,390 booking records using Python (Pandas, Matplotlib). Analyzed cancellation patterns across lead time, booking channel, deposit type, customer segment, and seasonality.ResultIdentified that lead time >120 days drives 52% cancellation rate and OTA channel cancels at 2.4× the rate of direct bookings. Delivered 6 actionable policy recommendations with quantified business impact.

Key Findings
MetricValueTotal Bookings Analyzed119,390Overall Cancellation Rate37.0%Cancellation Rate — Lead Time >120 Days52.2%Cancellation Rate — OTA Channel36.7%Cancellation Rate — Direct Channel15.3%Cancellation Rate — No Deposit28.4%Cancellation Rate — Repeat Guests14.2%Estimated Total Revenue Lost€16.7 Million

Dashboard Pages
Page 1 — Overview
Show Image
Cancellation rate broken down by hotel type, market segment, and deposit type. City Hotel shows significantly higher cancellation rate than Resort Hotel. Groups segment and Online TA are the highest-risk channels.

Page 2 — Lead Time & Seasonality
Show Image
Cancellation rate rises sharply with lead time — bookings made more than 120 days in advance cancel at 52%. Peak cancellation months align with peak booking season (July–August), suggesting a waitlist opportunity. Repeat guests cancel at 3× lower rate than new guests.

Page 3 — Revenue Leakage & Recommendations
Show Image
Transient customer segment accounts for the largest share of revenue loss. Portugal (PRT) leads cancellations by country. Guests with zero special requests cancel at the highest rate — low engagement signals low commitment.

Business Recommendations
#RecommendationInsight Behind It1Implement non-refundable deposit for bookings made >90 days in advanceLead time >120 days = 52% cancel rate2Offer 5% discount for direct bookings to reduce OTA dependencyOTA cancels at 2.4× rate of direct3Create a waitlist system for July–August peak monthsHigh volume + high cancellation = lost inventory4Re-engagement campaign for guests with 0 special requestsZero-request guests show lowest commitment5Flag high-risk booking pattern: long lead + OTA + no depositCombination drives highest cancellation probability6Priority retention program for Transient customer segmentHighest revenue loss segment

Tools & Technologies

Python — Pandas, Matplotlib, Seaborn
Analysis — Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA), Descriptive Statistics
Techniques — Cohort segmentation, Revenue quantification, Pattern detection


Dataset

Source: Hotel Booking Demand Dataset — Kaggle
Records: 119,390 bookings
Period: July 2015 — August 2017
Hotels: City Hotel & Resort Hotel (Portugal)
Features: 32 columns including lead time, ADR, market segment, deposit type, customer type


